{"parentUuid":"d3367147-5f55-4257-aaf0-f46858ce5ed4","isSidechain":false,"type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DWU8HAoAcK8154qwGkT1sn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Fullstack-Dev-Test-Task\n     2→\n     3→This is a task to test potential candidate skills in Python + SQL + TypeScript.\n     4→\n     5→- **Assignment**: Add Role-Aware Access + Architecture Decisions + **Run the app**\n     6→- **Timebox**: Aim for up to 1 hour. If you cut scope, say what you cut and why.\n     7→\n     8→\n     9→## Table of Contents\n    10→\n    11→- [Goal](#goal)\n    12→- [Base Template](#base-template)\n    13→- [Suggested Time Allocation](#suggested-time-allocation)\n    14→- [Requirements](#requirements)\n    15→  - [1. Clone the Base Template](#1-clone-the-base-template)\n    16→  - [2. Roles and Authorization Surface](#2-roles-and-authorization-surface)\n    17→  - [3. Code Quality Expectations](#3-code-quality-expectations)\n    18→  - [4. Architecture & Documentation](#4-architecture--documentation)\n    19→  - [5. Non-Functional Requirements](#5-non-functional-requirements)\n    20→  - [6. UX Behavior](#6-ux-behavior)\n    21→  - [7. Developer UX](#7-developer-ux)\n    22→- [Constraints](#constraints)\n    23→- [What We Review](#what-we-review)\n    24→- [Submission](#submission)\n    25→\n    26→## Goal\n    27→\n    28→Add role-based access control (RBAC) to the existing Full-Stack FastAPI Template so that only authorized users can access sensitive endpoints and UI sections.\n    29→\n    30→**We prioritize clean, maintainable code over comprehensive test coverage or extensive documentation.**\n    31→\n    32→You may reuse any libraries already in the template.\n    33→\n    34→> **Note**: RBAC can be implemented with simple role checks or a small policy layer. Keep scope tight. Favor clarity over cleverness.\n    35→\n    36→## Base Template\n    37→\n    38→**Tech Stack**:\n    39→- **Backend**: FastAPI / SQLModel / PostgreSQL\n    40→- **Frontend**: React / TypeScript\n    41→\n    42→**Repository**: [full-stack-fastapi-template](https://github.com/fastapi/full-stack-fastapi-template/tree/master)\n    43→\n    44→## Suggested Time Allocation\n    45→\n    46→How we believe it is doable in a 1-hour timebox:\n    47→\n    48→| Activity | Time           | Priority |\n    49→|----------|----------------|----------|\n    50→| Understanding the codebase | 15 min         | High |\n    51→| Implementation (clear, maintainable code) | 25 mins | **Critical** |\n    52→| Testing (focused, critical paths) | 10 min         | High |\n    53→| Documentation (README updates) | 10 min         | Medium |\n    54→\n    55→**If running short on time:**\n    56→- ✓ **Prioritize**: Clear, working authorization code with consistent patterns\n    57→- ✓ **Then**: 3-5 well-chosen tests covering critical scenarios\n    58→- ⚠ **Cut if needed**: Extra features, comprehensive test coverage, diagrams\n    59→- ❌ **Don't cut**: Security checks, README setup instructions\n    60→\n    61→## Requirements\n    62→\n    63→### 1. Clone the Base Template\n    64→\n    65→Clone the repository: https://github.com/fastapi/full-stack-fastapi-template/tree/master\n    66→\n    67→### 2. Roles and Authorization Surface\n    68→\n    69→#### Implement the Following Roles\n    70→\n    71→| Role | Permissions |\n    72→|------|-------------|\n    73→| **admin** | Full access to user management and settings |\n    74→| **manager** | Can list users and view metrics, but not change global settings |\n    75→| **member** | Can only access their own profile and basic app features |\n    76→\n    77→#### Protect a Small but Realistic Surface\n    78→\n    79→- List users\n    80→- Create user\n    81→- View \"metrics/insights\" page (simple stub is acceptable)\n    82→- View and update own profile\n    83→\n    84→**Exact permission mapping is up to you.**\n    85→\n    86→State it clearly in your docs and enforce it consistently in the backend and frontend.\n    87→\n    88→#### Example Permission Matrix (Document Something Similar)\n    89→\n    90→| Action | admin | manager | member |\n    91→|--------|-------|---------|--------|\n    92→| List all users | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |\n    93→| Create user | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n    94→| View metrics | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |\n    95→| Update own profile | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n    96→| Update any profile | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n    97→\n    98→### 3. Code Quality Expectations\n    99→\n   100→**We prioritize maintainable, readable code over clever solutions.**\n   101→ \n   102→- **Clear naming**: Function/variable names that explain intent without comments\n   103→- **Single responsibility**: Small, focused functions\n   104→- **Easy to extend**: Adding a new role shouldn't require touching 10+ files\n   105→- **Self-documenting**: Code structure makes the authorization model obvious\n   106→\n   107→> **Key principle**: A teammate should understand your authorization model in 5 minutes by reading your code.\n   108→\n   109→### 4. Architecture & Documentation\n   110→\n   111→Document your implementation approach clearly but concisely.\n   112→\n   113→#### Required\n   114→\n   115→- [ ] **Permission matrix** in README showing which role can access what\n   116→- [ ] **Brief explanation** (2-4 paragraphs) of your authorization approach:\n   117→  - Where authorization checks live (middleware, dependencies, decorators?)\n   118→  - How roles are stored and validated\n   119→  - How frontend learns about user capabilities\n   120→- [ ] **Inline code comments** only for non-obvious authorization logic\n   121→\n   122→#### Optional (Bonus Points)\n   123→\n   124→- [ ] **1-2 Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)** for your most critical decisions\n   125→  - Use any simple ADR format (problem, options, decision, trade-offs)\n   126→  - 200-400 words each\n   127→  - Example topics: Why you chose your authorization pattern, where checks live, how the frontend handles permissions\n   128→- [ ] **Simple diagram** showing where auth/authz checks happen\n   129→  - Mermaid, C4-style, or hand-drawn PNG is fine\n   130→\n   131→**Philosophy**: We value clear thinking over formal documentation. \n   132→Your code should clearly explain your approach; that's usually sufficient.\n   133→RBAC implementation, though, usually has at least a few options to implement, hence an additional README will add value.\n   134→\n   135→### 5. Non-Functional Requirements\n   136→\n   137→Demonstrate you considered real-world constraints:\n   138→\n   139→#### 1. Maintainability (Critical)\n   140→\n   141→- Keep coupling low; use consistent patterns\n   142→- A teammate should understand your authorization logic in 5 minutes\n   143→\n   144→#### 2. Testability (Important)\n   145→\n   146→- Provide **focused backend tests** covering critical authorization paths\n   147→\n   148→> **Note**: Tests are required, but we prioritize **quality over quantity**. 3 well-chosen tests with clean code beat 20 tests with spaghetti code.\n   149→\n   150→### 3. UX Behavior\n   151→\n   152→- **The UI** should:\n   153→  - Hide navigation links/buttons that the user can't access\n   154→  - Show a friendly \"Forbidden\" or \"Access Denied\" message if navigating directly to unauthorized routes\n   155→  - Not just fail silently or show cryptic errors\n   156→\n   157→### 4. Developer UX\n   158→\n   159→Update the README with:\n   160→\n   161→- **How to run locally** (setup, dependencies, database)\n   162→- **How to seed test data** with at least one admin and one non-admin user\n   163→- **How to run tests**\n   164→- **Database migrations** for any schema changes (if applicable)\n   165→\n   166→Make it easy for us to run your solution without hunting for setup instructions.\n   167→\n   168→## What We Review\n   169→\n   170→### Primary Criteria (60%)\n   171→\n   172→**Code readability and maintainability**\n   173→- ✓ Clear separation of concerns\n   174→- ✓ Consistent authorization patterns\n   175→- ✓ Self-documenting code structure\n   176→- ✓ Low coupling between components\n   177→- ✓ Easy to understand and extend\n   178→\n   179→**Working RBAC implementation**\n   180→- ✓ Consistent enforcement in backend and frontend\n   181→- ✓ No obvious security gaps or privilege escalation\n   182→- ✓ Correct HTTP status codes and error handling\n   183→\n   184→### Secondary Criteria (30%)\n   185→\n   186→**Test coverage**\n   187→- ✓ Focused tests on critical authorization paths\n   188→- ✓ Both allowed and denied scenarios tested\n   189→- ✓ Tests are clear and well-named\n   190→\n   191→**Setup and documentation**\n   192→- ✓ Setup instructions work on first try\n   193→- ✓ Clear explanation of authorization approach\n   194→- ✓ Permission matrix documented\n   195→\n   196→### Nice to Have (10%)\n   197→\n   198→- Thoughtful UX for forbidden states\n   199→- Observability (logging denied attempts)\n   200→- Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)\n   201→- Helpful diagrams\n   202→- Extra polish\n   203→\n   204→> **Philosophy**: We're evaluating your ability to write production-quality code under time constraints. We'd rather hire someone who delivers clean, working code with good tests than someone who delivers everything but it's hard to maintain.\n   205→\n   206→## Submission\n   207→\n   208→**Deliverables**:\n   209→\n   210→- [ ] PR or repo link with commit history\n   211→- [ ] Updated README with:\n   212→  - Setup instructions\n   213→  - Permission matrix\n   214→  - Brief explanation of your approach\n   215→- [ ] Backend tests covering critical authorization scenarios\n   216→- [ ] Working implementation of RBAC\n   217→- [ ] Optional: `NOTES.md` with anything you want us to know (scope cuts, trade-offs, what you'd do with more time)\n   218→\n   219→---\n   220→\n   221→**Good luck!** Focus on demonstrating clear thinking and solid engineering fundamentals. We're looking for maintainable code, not perfect code.\n   222→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"10919125-e1af-4025-b5c0-1b74592c3a58","timestamp":"2026-08-30T14:55:10.871Z"},"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"","agentId":"a81bed5c18197e77f"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_011CeZ8F24y5Ee9kiprHmajk","parentToolUseID":"toolu_013crcSWRNmFYZEEHUGgS3Xh","uuid":"523ed13c-29c0-45cb-9e61-84d11c31daa0","timestamp":"2026-08-30T14:55:11.667Z","userType":"external","cwd":"/home/dimakhaver/programming/EnderTuring/rbac-fastapi-task/frontend","sessionId":"4a1f3817-2385-4e23-95d7-028e997c9847","version":"2.1.74","gitBranch":"feature/rbac","slug":"jaunty-dancing-liskov"}
{"parentUuid":"523ed13c-29c0-45cb-9e61-84d11c31daa0","isSidechain":false,"type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"assistant","timestamp":"2026-08-30T14:55:11.668Z","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CeZ8FyUUtKnBPAiwxvhHw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RJVEgXzWqXGBCmntKLQNDt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/dimakhaver/programming/EnderTuring/rbac-fastapi-task/docs/plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10679,"cache_read_input_tokens":877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10679,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"requestId":"req_011CeZ8Fxe8BipixssUPPiGg","uuid":"583cee93-87b6-4e53-a07f-7f959956062d"},"type":"agent_progress","prompt":"","agentId":"a81bed5c18197e77f"},"toolUseID":"agent_msg_011CeZ8F24y5Ee9kiprHmajk","parentToolUseID":"toolu_013crcSWRNmFYZEEHUGgS3Xh","uuid":"d98cb95c-81f9-4c47-be1f-f4005c07bc33","timestamp":"2026-08-30T14:55:11.668Z","userType":"external","cwd":"/home/dimakhaver/programming/EnderTuring/rbac-fastapi-task/frontend","sessionId":"4a1f3817-2385-4e23-95d7-028e997c9847","version":"2.1.74","gitBranch":"feature/rbac","slug":"jaunty-dancing-liskov"}
{"parentUuid":"d98cb95c-81f9-4c47-be1f-f4005c07bc33","isSidechain":false,"type":"progress","data":{"message":{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RJVEgXzWqXGBCmntKLQNDt","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# RBAC Implementation Plan\n     2→\n     3→> **For agentic workers:** executed inline in this session (superpowers:executing-plans), TDD per milestone, one conventional commit per milestone, `uv run prek run --all-files` green before every commit.\n     4→\n     5→**Goal:** Role-based access control (admin / manager / member) enforced in the backend via one dependency factory, mirrored as UX-only guards in the frontend.\n     6→\n     7→**Architecture:** Single `role` enum column on `User` (source of truth; `is_superuser` kept for template compatibility). One `require_roles(*roles)` dependency in `app/api/deps.py`; central action→roles mapping in `app/api/permissions.py`; routes never contain role literals. Frontend reads `role` from `UserPublic` and mirrors the mapping in `src/lib/roles.ts`.\n     8→\n     9→**Tech stack:** FastAPI, SQLModel, Alembic, PostgreSQL enum; React + TanStack Router; openapi-ts generated client.\n    10→\n    11→## Global constraints\n    12→\n    13→- 401 = missing/invalid credentials; 403 = authenticated but not permitted. Never mix.\n    14→- Every denial: `logger.warning` with user id + route.\n    15→- No inline role literals in route bodies; adding a role touches ≤3 files (enum, permissions map, migration).\n    16→- Tests: parametrized role×endpoint, allowed AND denied cases, plus 401 unauthenticated. Few and sharp.\n    17→- Frontend verified by lint/typecheck/build only (no e2e).\n    18→\n    19→## Permission matrix (single source: `app/api/permissions.py`)\n    20→\n    21→| Action | Constant | admin | manager | member |\n    22→|---|---|---|---|---|\n    23→| List users `GET /users/` | `LIST_USERS` | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |\n    24→| Create user `POST /users/` | `MANAGE_USERS` | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n    25→| Update/delete any user `PATCH/DELETE /users/{id}` | `MANAGE_USERS` | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n    26→| Read other user `GET /users/{id}` | `READ_ANY_USER` | ✓ | ✓ | self only |\n    27→| Metrics `GET /metrics/summary` | `VIEW_METRICS` | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |\n    28→| Global settings `GET/PUT /settings/` | `MANAGE_SETTINGS` | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |\n    29→| Own profile `GET/PATCH /users/me` | (authenticated) | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n    30→| `POST /users/signup` | public; always assigns `member` | — | — | — |\n    31→\n    32→---\n    33→\n    34→## Milestone 1 — `feat: add role column to user model with migration`\n    35→\n    36→**Files:** modify `backend/app/models.py`, `backend/app/core/db.py`; create `backend/app/alembic/versions/<rev>_add_role_to_user.py` (down_revision `fe56fa70289e`).\n    37→\n    38→- `models.py`: add\n    39→\n    40→  ```python\n    41→  class UserRole(str, enum.Enum):\n    42→      admin = \"admin\"      # lowercase members so SQLAlchemy's name-based\n    43→      manager = \"manager\"  # enum storage matches the value\n    44→      member = \"member\"\n    45→  ```\n    46→\n    47→  `UserBase` gains `role: UserRole = Field(default=UserRole.member)` (flows to `UserCreate` — admin endpoint — and `UserPublic` — frontend). `UserUpdate` gains `role: UserRole | None = None`. `UserRegister` and `UserUpdateMe` unchanged → no self-escalation path.\n    48→- `core/db.py` `init_db`: first superuser gets `role=UserRole.admin`.\n    49→- Migration (`uv run alembic revision -m \"add role to user\"`, then fill):\n    50→\n    51→  ```python\n    52→  def upgrade():\n    53→      user_role = sa.Enum(\"admin\", \"manager\", \"member\", name=\"userrole\")\n    54→      user_role.create(op.get_bind())\n    55→      op.add_column(\"user\", sa.Column(\"role\", user_role, nullable=False,\n    56→                                      server_default=\"member\"))\n    57→      op.execute(\"\"\"UPDATE \"user\" SET role = 'admin' WHERE is_superuser = true\"\"\")\n    58→\n    59→  def downgrade():\n    60→      op.drop_column(\"user\", \"role\")\n    61→      sa.Enum(name=\"userrole\").drop(op.get_bind())\n    62→  ```\n    63→- Verify: `uv run alembic upgrade head`, run test suite (still green), `uv run alembic downgrade -1 && uv run alembic upgrade head` round-trip. Commit.\n    64→\n    65→## Milestone 2 — `feat: enforce role-based authorization on user routes`\n    66→\n    67→**Files:** create `backend/app/api/permissions.py`; modify `backend/app/api/deps.py`, `backend/app/api/routes/users.py`. Failing tests from Milestone 3 are written FIRST (kept uncommitted until M3), shown red, then this milestone makes them green.\n    68→\n    69→- `permissions.py`:\n    70→\n    71→  ```python\n    72→  from app.models import UserRole\n    73→\n    74→  LIST_USERS = (UserRole.admin, UserRole.manager)\n    75→  MANAGE_USERS = (UserRole.admin,)\n    76→  READ_ANY_USER = (UserRole.admin, UserRole.manager)\n    77→  VIEW_METRICS = (UserRole.admin, UserRole.manager)\n    78→  MANAGE_SETTINGS = (UserRole.admin,)\n    79→  ```\n    80→- `deps.py`: `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`; invalid-JWT branch in `get_current_user` becomes **401** with `headers={\"WWW-Authenticate\": \"Bearer\"}` (was 403); add:\n    81→\n    82→  ```python\n    83→  def ensure_role(user: User, allowed: tuple[UserRole, ...], route: str) -> None:\n    84→      if user.role not in allowed:\n    85→          logger.warning(\"Access denied: user=%s role=%s route=%s\",\n    86→                         user.id, user.role.value, route)\n    87→          raise HTTPException(status_code=403,\n    88→                              detail=\"The user doesn't have enough privileges\")\n    89→\n    90→  def require_roles(*allowed: UserRole) -> Callable[..., User]:\n    91→      def check_role(current_user: CurrentUser, request: Request) -> User:\n    92→          ensure_role(current_user, allowed, request.url.path)\n    93→          return current_user\n    94→      return check_role\n    95→  ```\n    96→- `users.py`: swap `Depends(get_current_active_superuser)` → `Depends(require_roles(*permissions.LIST_USERS))` on `read_users`; `MANAGE_USERS` on `create_user`, `update_user`, `delete_user`. `read_user_by_id`: self OR `ensure_role(current_user, permissions.READ_ANY_USER, request.url.path)`. Self-delete guards (`users.py:137`, `users.py:224`) switch `is_superuser` → `role == UserRole.admin` (role is source of truth), messages unchanged. `get_current_active_superuser` stays for out-of-surface routes (utils test-email, private) — NOTES.md.\n    97→- Verify: new tests green, full suite green. Commit (implementation only).\n    98→\n    99→## Milestone 3 — `test: role-based authorization matrix`\n   100→\n   101→**Files:** create `backend/tests/api/routes/test_rbac.py`; modify `backend/tests/conftest.py`, `backend/tests/utils/user.py`. (Written during M2 TDD; committed here.)\n   102→\n   103→- `tests/utils/user.py`: `authentication_token_from_email(..., role: UserRole = UserRole.member)` — create user with that role, or update existing user's password AND role.\n   104→- `conftest.py`: add module-scoped `manager_token_headers` (email `manager@example.com`, `role=UserRole.manager`).\n   105→- `test_rbac.py` — fixture map `{\"admin\": \"superuser_token_headers\", \"manager\": \"manager_token_headers\", \"member\": \"normal_user_token_headers\"}`; tests:\n   106→  1. `test_role_matrix` — parametrized over (method, path, body, role, expected): `GET /users/` (admin 200, manager 200, member 403), `POST /users/` with random email/password (admin 200, manager 403, member 403), `GET /users/{other_id}` (manager 200, member 403).\n   107→  2. `test_protected_endpoint_without_token_returns_401`\n   108→  3. `test_invalid_token_returns_401` (garbage bearer token)\n   109→  4. `test_member_can_read_and_update_own_profile` (`GET /users/me`, `PATCH /users/me` → 200)\n   110→  5. `test_member_cannot_escalate_own_role` (`PATCH /users/me` with `\"role\": \"admin\"` in body → role stays `member`)\n   111→  6. `test_signup_assigns_member_role`\n   112→- Verify red→green already shown in M2; full suite green. Commit tests.\n   113→\n   114→## Milestone 4 — `feat: add metrics summary and global settings stub endpoints`\n   115→\n   116→**Files:** create `backend/app/api/routes/metrics.py`, `backend/app/api/routes/app_settings.py`; modify `backend/app/models.py` (add `MetricsSummary`, `GlobalSettings`), `backend/app/api/main.py`, `backend/tests/api/routes/test_rbac.py` (matrix rows for the new endpoints, written first, shown red).\n   117→\n   118→- `metrics.py`: `GET /metrics/summary`, `dependencies=[Depends(require_roles(*permissions.VIEW_METRICS))]`, returns `MetricsSummary(total_users, active_users, users_by_role)` via `func.count` queries.\n   119→- `app_settings.py`: module named to avoid clashing with `core.config.settings`; `GET /settings/` + `PUT /settings/` guarded by `MANAGE_SETTINGS`; in-memory module-level `GlobalSettings(app_name=..., maintenance_mode=False)` store — stub, not persisted (NOTES.md).\n   120→- Matrix rows: metrics (admin 200, manager 200, member 403); settings GET+PUT (admin 200, manager 403, member 403).\n   121→- Verify red → implement → green. Commit.\n   122→\n   123→## Milestone 5 — `feat: role-aware frontend guards, nav, and metrics page`\n   124→\n   125→**Files:** regenerate `frontend/src/client/`; create `frontend/src/lib/roles.ts`, `frontend/src/routes/_layout/forbidden.tsx`, `frontend/src/routes/_layout/metrics.tsx`; modify `frontend/src/routes/_layout/admin.tsx`, `frontend/src/components/Sidebar/AppSidebar.tsx`, `frontend/src/components/Admin/columns.tsx` (Role column shows `role` badge; actions column filtered out for non-admin via component), admin route component (AddUser only for admin).\n   126→\n   127→- `bash scripts/generate-client.sh` from repo root; confirm `UserPublic.role` exists.\n   128→- `roles.ts` mirrors backend mapping (UX only — backend authoritative):\n   129→\n   130→  ```ts\n   131→  import